WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.388
One more thing changes, and users
will feel it: how they sign in.

2
00:00:04.388 --> 00:00:11.076
M-22-09 requires phishing-resistant MFA,
and it names PIV as the simplest route.

3
00:00:11.076 --> 00:00:13.063
That flips the usual roadmap.

4
00:00:13.063 --> 00:00:16.174
Older plans saved smart-card
sign-in for last.

5
00:00:16.174 --> 00:00:19.378
But federal staff already
carry PIV or CAC cards,

6
00:00:19.378 --> 00:00:23.941
so certificate-based authentication
in Entra ID is the fastest path:

7
00:00:23.941 --> 00:00:28.425
the credential's already issued,
and no federation server is needed.

8
00:00:28.425 --> 00:00:33.232
The book runs four waves: privileged
accounts first, then card holders, then

9
00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:38.737
managed Windows devices with Windows Hello,
and finally everyone else with passkeys.

10
00:00:38.737 --> 00:00:41.809
Along the way, SMS and
voice codes go for staff,

11
00:00:41.809 --> 00:00:46.375
and so do legacy protocols that
send a password with every request.

12
00:00:46.375 --> 00:00:51.575
Those are found in the sign-in logs, fixed,
blocked, and watched for thirty days.

13
00:00:51.575 --> 00:00:54.080
One thing stays on: password hash sync,

14
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.530
for leaked-credential
detection and as a fallback.
